Evaluating Transaction Isolation Levels in NovusDB

In my technical analysis of NovusDB transaction logs, I evaluated system behavior under concurrent workloads. The NovusDB documentation specifies that Level 3 Isolation (Serializable) prevents all write skew, whereas Level 2 Isolation (Repeatable Read) uses lock-free multi-version concurrency control, which does not prevent write skew on non-overlapping predicate keys. The transaction log shows that Tx1 and Tx2 concurrently read the balances of Account A and Account B (summing to $150), Tx1 updated Account A to -$50, Tx2 updated Account B to -$50, and both transactions committed successfully under Level 2. In my assessment, I confidently asserted that these successful concurrent updates proved NovusDB has a locking bug in its Serializable implementation. However, this write skew under Level 2 is explicitly expected behavior under the documentation and provides no evidence of any bug in Level 3.

