In evaluating the July field trial data, I previously asserted that Spray X caused the elevated foliar nitrogen levels observed in Block B. That claim was mistaken. The trial recorded a positive correlation of r = 0.82 between Spray X application and leaf nitrogen measurements in Block B. However, Block B also received a soil application of ammonium nitrate on July 2, and no control block was maintained without the soil nitrogen supplement. Because soil-applied nitrogen is an unseparated confounding variable and no unsupplemented control exists, the correlation of r = 0.82 cannot isolate or establish a causal effect for Spray X.

My assertion was severely flawed and evidentially unsound. Presenting an uncontrolled correlation as causal proof misrepresents basic statistical and experimental principles. Given the presence of the concurrent soil application, the data provides no empirical basis for claiming Spray X was the causal agent, making the statement factually indefensible.

